  "headline": "What Mistakes Should I Avoid When Developing a Custom Tiny Home Floor Plan?",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "Zen Tiny Homes"
  },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "Zen Tiny Homes",
    "url": "www.zentinyhomes.com"
  },
  "articleBody": "Creating a custom floor plan for your tiny home is exciting, but it's easy to overlook crucial details that can impact livability. To ensure your design truly shines, steer clear of these common blunders. One big mistake is neglecting the golden rule of multi-purpose spaces. In a tiny home, every square foot should ideally serve more than one function. If your dining table is *just* a dining table, you might be wasting valuable space that could also be a desk or prep area. Another error is failing to consider natural light and ventilation early in the process. Good lighting and airflow make a tiny space feel much larger and more comfortable, so position windows strategically. Don't forget about storage – it's often an afterthought, but it should be integrated seamlessly into the design. Think about under-stair drawers, built-in shelving, and vertical solutions from the outset. A common misstep is not planning for future needs. Even if you're single now, will you need space for a partner, occasional guests, or a hobby down the line? Building in flexibility can prevent costly changes later. Lastly, many clients try to scale down a traditional home layout rather than starting fresh with a 'tiny-first' mentality. This usually leads to cramped, inefficient designs. Zen Tiny Homes excels at designing from the ground up, focusing on compact living realities, ensuring your custom plan is intentional and high-functioning.",
